<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-integration-techniques">
    <title>Techniques of Integration</title>

    <section xml:id="sec-substitution">
        <title>The Substitution Rule</title>

        <p>
            Antidifferentiation is often more difficult than differentiation.
            One useful strategy is to recognize when an integrand has been
            produced by the chain rule and reverse that process.
        </p>

        <p>
            Recall that the chain rule gives
        </p>

        <me>
            \frac{d}{dx}F(g(x))
            =
            F'(g(x))g'(x).
        </me>

        <p>
            Reversing this relationship leads to the substitution rule:
        </p>

        <me>
            \int F'(g(x))g'(x)\,dx
            =
            F(g(x))+C.
        </me>

        <p>
            We introduce a new variable
        </p>

        <me>
            u=g(x),
        </me>

        <p>
            so that
        </p>

        <me>
            du=g'(x)\,dx.
        </me>

        <p>
            The goal is to rewrite the entire integral in terms of
            <m>u</m>.
        </p>

        <theorem xml:id="thm-substitution-indefinite">
            <title>Substitution Rule for Indefinite Integrals</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>u=g(x)</m> is differentiable, then
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int f(g(x))g'(x)\,dx
                    =
                    \int f(u)\,du.
                </me>
            </statement>
        </theorem>

        <remark xml:id="rem-substitution-chain-rule">
            <title>Substitution reverses the chain rule</title>

            <p>
                A useful way to identify a substitution is to look for a
                function inside another function together with its derivative,
                possibly up to a constant factor.
            </p>
        </remark>

        <example xml:id="ex-substitution-basic">
            <title>A basic substitution</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Evaluate
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int 2x\cos(x^2)\,dx.
                </me>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    The expression <m>x^2</m> appears inside the cosine, and
                    its derivative is <m>2x</m>. Let
                </p>

                <me>
                    u=x^2.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Then
                </p>

                <me>
                    du=2x\,dx.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Therefore,
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int 2x\cos(x^2)\,dx
                    =
                    \int \cos u\,du.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Integrating,
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int \cos u\,du
                    =
                    \sin u+C.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Substituting back,
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int 2x\cos(x^2)\,dx
                    =
                    \sin(x^2)+C.
                </me>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="ex-substitution-exp">
            <title>Substitution with an exponential</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Evaluate
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int x e^{x^2}\,dx.
                </me>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Let
                </p>

                <me>
                    u=x^2.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Then
                </p>

                <me>
                    du=2x\,dx,
                </me>

                <p>
                    so
                </p>

                <me>
                    x\,dx=\frac{1}{2}\,du.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Thus,
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int x e^{x^2}\,dx
                    =
                    \frac{1}{2}\int e^u\,du.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Therefore,
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int x e^{x^2}\,dx
                    =
                    \frac{1}{2}e^u+C
                    =
                    \frac{1}{2}e^{x^2}+C.
                </me>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="ex-substitution-power">
            <title>Substitution with a power</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Evaluate
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int x(x^2+1)^5\,dx.
                </me>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Let
                </p>

                <me>
                    u=x^2+1.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Then
                </p>

                <me>
                    du=2x\,dx,
                </me>

                <p>
                    and hence
                </p>

                <me>
                    x\,dx=\frac{1}{2}\,du.
                </me>

                <p>
                    The integral becomes
                </p>

                <me>
                    \frac{1}{2}\int u^5\,du.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Therefore,
                </p>

                <me>
                    \frac{1}{2}\int u^5\,du
                    =
                    \frac{1}{12}u^6+C.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Substituting back gives
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int x(x^2+1)^5\,dx
                    =
                    \frac{1}{12}(x^2+1)^6+C.
                </me>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="ex-substitution-log">
            <title>Substitution leading to a logarithm</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Evaluate
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int \frac{x}{x^2+4}\,dx.
                </me>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Let
                </p>

                <me>
                    u=x^2+4.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Then
                </p>

                <me>
                    du=2x\,dx.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Thus,
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int \frac{x}{x^2+4}\,dx
                    =
                    \frac{1}{2}
                    \int \frac{1}{u}\,du.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Therefore,
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int \frac{x}{x^2+4}\,dx
                    =
                    \frac{1}{2}\ln|u|+C.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Since <m>x^2+4&gt;0</m>, this may be written as
                </p>

                <me>
                    \frac{1}{2}\ln(x^2+4)+C.
                </me>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <remark xml:id="rem-substitution-choice">
            <title>Choosing <m>u</m></title>

            <p>
                There is no single rule that always tells us which
                substitution to choose. A good first choice is often an
                expression that appears inside another function, especially
                when its derivative also appears elsewhere in the integrand.
            </p>
        </remark>

        <example xml:id="ex-substitution-rewrite">
            <title>Rewriting before substituting</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Evaluate
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int \frac{x^3}{x^2+1}\,dx.
                </me>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    The expression <m>x^2+1</m> suggests the substitution
                </p>

                <me>
                    u=x^2+1.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Since
                </p>

                <me>
                    du=2x\,dx,
                </me>

                <p>
                    we first rewrite
                </p>

                <me>
                    x^3\,dx
                    =
                    x^2(x\,dx).
                </me>

                <p>
                    Also, from <m>u=x^2+1</m>,
                </p>

                <me>
                    x^2=u-1.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Therefore,
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int \frac{x^3}{x^2+1}\,dx
                    =
                    \frac{1}{2}
                    \int \frac{u-1}{u}\,du.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Simplifying,
                </p>

                <me>
                    \frac{1}{2}
                    \int
                    \left(
                    1-\frac{1}{u}
                    \right)\,du.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Hence,
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int \frac{x^3}{x^2+1}\,dx
                    =
                    \frac{1}{2}
                    \left(
                    u-\ln|u|
                    \right)+C.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Substituting back,
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int \frac{x^3}{x^2+1}\,dx
                    =
                    \frac{1}{2}
                    \left(
                    x^2+1-\ln(x^2+1)
                    \right)+C.
                </me>
            </solution>
        </example>

    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-substitution-definite">
        <title>Substitution in Definite Integrals</title>

        <p>
            The substitution rule can also be used with definite integrals.
            There are two valid approaches.
        </p>

        <p>
            We can either perform the substitution, integrate in terms of
            <m>u</m>, then substitute back to <m>x</m> before evaluating the
            original bounds, or we can change the bounds to the corresponding
            <m>u</m>-values and complete the entire calculation in terms of
            <m>u</m>.
        </p>

        <theorem xml:id="thm-substitution-definite">
            <title>Substitution Rule for Definite Integrals</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>u=g(x)</m>, then
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int_a^b f(g(x))g'(x)\,dx
                    =
                    \int_{g(a)}^{g(b)} f(u)\,du.
                </me>
            </statement>
        </theorem>

        <example xml:id="ex-substitution-definite-basic">
            <title>Changing the bounds</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Evaluate
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int_0^1 2x e^{x^2}\,dx.
                </me>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Let
                </p>

                <me>
                    u=x^2,
                </me>

                <p>
                    so that
                </p>

                <me>
                    du=2x\,dx.
                </me>

                <p>
                    We now change the limits. When <m>x=0</m>,
                </p>

                <me>
                    u=0,
                </me>

                <p>
                    and when <m>x=1</m>,
                </p>

                <me>
                    u=1.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Therefore,
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int_0^1 2x e^{x^2}\,dx
                    =
                    \int_0^1 e^u\,du.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Hence,
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int_0^1 e^u\,du
                    =
                    [e^u]_0^1
                    =
                    e-1.
                </me>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="ex-substitution-definite-polynomial">
            <title>A definite integral with new bounds</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Evaluate
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int_0^2 x(x^2+1)^2\,dx.
                </me>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Let
                </p>

                <me>
                    u=x^2+1.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Then
                </p>

                <me>
                    du=2x\,dx,
                </me>

                <p>
                    so
                </p>

                <me>
                    x\,dx=\frac{1}{2}\,du.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Change the bounds:
                </p>

                <me>
                    x=0
                    \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
                    u=1,
                </me>

                <me>
                    x=2
                    \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
                    u=5.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Therefore,
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int_0^2 x(x^2+1)^2\,dx
                    =
                    \frac{1}{2}
                    \int_1^5 u^2\,du.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Evaluating,
                </p>

                <me>
                    \frac{1}{2}
                    \left[
                    \frac{u^3}{3}
                    \right]_1^5
                    =
                    \frac{1}{6}(125-1)
                    =
                    \frac{62}{3}.
                </me>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <remark xml:id="rem-definite-substitution-bounds">
            <title>Do not mix variables and bounds</title>

            <p>
                Once the integral has been rewritten entirely in terms of
                <m>u</m>, the limits of integration should also be
                <m>u</m>-values. Alternatively, substitute back to
                <m>x</m> before using the original <m>x</m>-limits.
            </p>
        </remark>

        <example xml:id="ex-substitution-definite-trig">
            <title>A trigonometric definite integral</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Evaluate
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int_0^{\pi/2}
                    \sin x\,e^{\cos x}\,dx.
                </me>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Let
                </p>

                <me>
                    u=\cos x.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Then
                </p>

                <me>
                    du=-\sin x\,dx.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Change the bounds:
                </p>

                <me>
                    x=0
                    \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
                    u=1,
                </me>

                <me>
                    x=\frac{\pi}{2}
                    \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
                    u=0.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Therefore,
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int_0^{\pi/2}
                    \sin x\,e^{\cos x}\,dx
                    =
                    -\int_1^0 e^u\,du.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Reversing the limits,
                </p>

                <me>
                    -\int_1^0 e^u\,du
                    =
                    \int_0^1 e^u\,du.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Hence,
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int_0^{\pi/2}
                    \sin x\,e^{\cos x}\,dx
                    =
                    e-1.
                </me>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <remark xml:id="rem-substitution-steps">
            <title>A useful substitution workflow</title>

            <p>
                When using substitution, the following sequence is often
                helpful:
            </p>

            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Choose an expression for <m>u</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Compute <m>du</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Rewrite the entire integrand in terms of <m>u</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        If the integral is definite, change the bounds to
                        <m>u</m>-values.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Integrate and simplify.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </remark>

    </section>

